  "id": "DrugBank_DB00620 - MONDO_2350",
  "verbalization": "Tiamcinolone, a corticosteroid, is implicated in the treatment of Familial Nephrotic Syndrome through a chain of indications that ultimately lead to its use in this condition. The evidence suggests that Triamcinolone is used in conjunction with Methylprednisolone, a corticosteroid with similar therapeutic effects, to manage conditions such as Sarcoidosis and Familial Adrenal Hypoplasia With Absent Pituitary Luteinizing Hormone. These conditions, in turn, are linked to Familial Nephrotic Syndrome, indicating a potential shared pathophysiological mechanism involving corticosteroid therapy. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of corticosteroids in modulating immune and renal functions, which may contribute to the management of nephrotic syndrome. The recurring use of Methylprednisolone as a bridge between these conditions suggests a possible overlap in the biological processes underlying these disorders, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
